3203125, -0.0090484619140625, -0.015960693359375, 0.0044708251953125, 0.025115966796875, 0.048004150390625, 0.0338134765625, 0.0163726806640625, 0.005985260009765625, 0.033172607421875, 0.00978851318359375, -0.059295654296875, 0.0006475448608398438, 0.00499725341796875, 0.029571533203125, -0.004085540771484375, -0.0106353759765625, 0.01873779296875, -0.0127716064453125, -0.043975830078125, 0.01401519775390625, -0.01514434814453125, 0.00960540771484375, -0.00531005859375, -0.0294342041015625, -0.02886962890625, 0.00952911376953125, -0.0161895751953125, -0.0223846435546875, 0.01300048828125, -0.0145111083984375, 0.026336669921875, 0.00726318359375, -0.03948974609375, -0.015045166015625, -0.0014190673828125, 0.00782012939453125, -0.017791748046875, 0.018798828125, 0.00823974609375, -0.0021839141845703125, -0.00021839141845703125, -0.0245513916015625, 0.0027065277099609375, 0.033416748046875, -0.00623321533203125, 0.0054473876953125, 0.01385498046875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Recent publications have begun to explore the application of Geometric Algebra (GA) to the modeling, analysis, and control of dynamical systems, particularly in the context of electrical circuits [1,2]. However, a central challenge remains: the transformation of the ordinary differential equations that govern a system's behavior from the real domain to the Laplace domain, a process fundamental to system analysis and control design. Existing approaches to Laplace transforms in hypercomplex algebras, such as quaternions [3] and Clifford algebras [4], are limited in several ways. Specifically, they often fail to provide a unified treatment of all multigrade components, do not fully address the non-commutative structure of GA, and may not support the simultaneous transformation of coupled scalar, vector, and higher-grade quantities. In practical applications, this leads to difficulties such as the need to split physical quantities into separate components, loss of geometric relationships, and increased complexity in analyzing systems with inherently multivector-valued signals (e.g., in multi-phase circuits or electromagnetic field analysis). Thus, a comprehensive and consistent definition of the Laplace transform within the framework of Geometric Algebra remains lacking, impeding both theoretical development and practical modeling. Our work addresses this gap by providing a formal definition and analysis of the Laplace transform for multivector-valued functions in GA, with explicit attention to the algebra\u2019s non-commutative structure and the preservation of geometric relationships.\n\nThe main contributions of this work are as follows: (1) we introduce a precise definition of the Laplace transform for multivector-valued functions in geometric algebras of signature (p, q) with p + q \u2264 5; (2) we establish key properties of this transform, including linearity, the shifting theorem, and the treatment of non-commutative products, and discuss the extent to which other classical properties (such as the convolution theorem and initial/final value theorems) generalize; (3) we clarify the relationship and improvements over previous approaches for quaternions and Clifford algebras, highlighting the ability to handle all grades and non-commutative products, and provide a detailed comparison of technical differences and practical consequences; and (4) we demonstrate the practical benefits of our approach through a concrete example involving RLC circuit analysis, showing how geometric relationships are preserved and how the transform is applied in practice.\n\nOur definition of the Laplace transform in GA is as follows: for a multivector-valued function \\( f: [0, \\infty) \\to \\mathcal{G}_{p,q} \\), where \\( \\mathcal{G}_{p,q} \\) is a geometric algebra of signature (p, q), the Laplace transform \\( \\mathcal{L}\\{f\\}(s) \\) is defined by\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}\\{f\\}(s) = \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} f(t) \\, dt,\n\\]\nwhere \\( s \\) is a real or complex parameter. The exponential \\( e^{-st} \\) is interpreted as a scalar function of \\( t \\), and thus acts as a scalar multiplier on each grade component of \\( f(t) \\): if \\( f(t) = \\sum_{k=0}^n \\langle f(t) \\rangle_k \\), then\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}\\{f\\}(s) = \\sum_{k=0}^n \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} \\langle f(t) \\rangle_k \\, dt.\n\\]\nThis definition is chosen because it is the most direct and natural extension of the classical Laplace transform to multivector-valued functions: it applies the transform independently to each grade, preserving linearity and the algebraic structure. Alternative definitions, such as allowing \\( s \\) to be a general multivector or defining the exponential via the geometric product, were considered but found to introduce ambiguities and loss of key properties (see below). Our approach is unique in that it ensures the transform is well-defined, grade-preserving, and compatible with the non-commutative structure of GA.\n\nSince \\( e^{-st} \\) is scalar-valued for real or complex \\( s \\), it commutes with all multivector components, ensuring the integral is well-defined. For complex \\( s \\), the scalar exponential remains commutative with all elements of \\( \\mathcal{G}_{p,q} \\) [see, e.g., 1, Ch. 2]. If \\( s \\) were allowed to be a general multivector, the non-commutativity of the geometric product would introduce ambiguities in the definition of the exponential and the integral. For example, if \\( s = a + \\mathbf{v} \\) with \\( \\mathbf{v} \\) a vector, then \\( e^{-st} \\) would involve terms like \\( e^{-a t} e^{-\\mathbf{v} t} \\), but \\( e^{-\\mathbf{v} t} \\) does not, in general, commute with multivector components of \\( f(t) \\), and the order of multiplication becomes ambiguous. This can lead to different results depending on the order of operations, and may even make the integral ill-defined. Therefore, our definition restricts \\( s \\) to scalar or complex values to avoid such issues. While this restriction may limit certain theoretical generalizations (e.g., Laplace transforms with multivector parameters), it does not affect the vast majority of practical applications, where the Laplace parameter is interpreted as a frequency or complex variable.\n\nWe focus on geometric algebras with signature (p, q) where p + q \u2264 5. This restriction is motivated primarily by computational tractability: as p + q increases, the dimension of the algebra grows exponentially (e.g., for p + q = 5, the algebra has 32 dimensions), making both analytical and numerical computations increasingly impractical. Importantly, the main properties and theorems established for our Laplace transform\u2014such as linearity, shifting, and grade-wise application\u2014hold for arbitrary finite-dimensional GAs, as the definition and proofs do not depend on the specific dimension. The restriction to p + q \u2264 5 is thus purely practical, not theoretical, and encompasses the most common physical and engineering applications, such as the spacetime algebra (1,3) and the Euclidean algebra (3,0).\n\nOur approach generalizes previous work on quaternions [3] and Clifford algebras [4] by providing a unified treatment for all grades in GA, rather than focusing on specific subalgebras or assuming commutativity. For clarity, we summarize the main differences below:\n\n- **Quaternions [3]**: Limited to four-dimensional algebras; typically treats only scalar and vector parts; does not address higher-grade components; assumes commutativity between the Laplace parameter and quaternionic elements.\n- **Clifford algebras [4]**: Often assumes monogenic functions or restricts to scalar-valued transforms; may require commutativity or focus on specific subalgebras; does not provide a general framework for arbitrary multivector-valued functions.\n- **This work**: Applies to arbitrary multivector-valued functions in GA; handles all grades simultaneously; explicitly addresses non-commutativity by restricting the Laplace parameter to scalars or complex numbers; preserves geometric relationships and enables unified analysis of coupled components.\n\nA key challenge in transforming multivector-valued functions is the non-commutative nature of the geometric product. Our definition ensures that the Laplace transform is applied linearly and grade-wise, so that the relationships between grades are preserved. The scalar exponential \\( e^{-st} \\) commutes with all multivector components, as justified above, so the transform is well-defined for all admissible functions. If the Laplace parameter \\( s \\) were a general multivector, the exponential would not, in general, commute with multivector components, leading to ambiguities; thus, our restriction on \\( s \\) is essential for consistency.\n\nRegarding properties, our GA Laplace transform preserves several classical properties:\n\n- **Linearity**: The transform is linear over the real or complex field.\n- **Shifting theorem**: The standard time-shifting property holds grade-wise.\n- **Convolution theorem**: The convolution theorem generalizes to the GA context, provided the convolution is defined grade-wise and the product is interpreted as the geometric product; care must be taken with non-commutative products.\n- **Initial and final value theorems**: These theorems hold for each grade component, provided the limits exist.\n- **Differentiation and integration**: The Laplace transform of the derivative and integral of a multivector-valued function follows the classical pattern, applied to each grade.\n\nHowever, properties that rely on commutativity (e.g., certain factorization results) may not generalize directly and require careful interpretation in the GA setting.\n\nAs with the classical Laplace transform, not all functions admit a Laplace transform in GA. The class of admissible functions consists of those whose grade components are of exponential order and piecewise continuous on every finite interval. For multivector-valued functions, admissibility is determined by the \"worst\" grade: if any grade component fails to be of exponential order or is not piecewise continuous, the Laplace transform of the entire function does not exist. For example, if \\( f(t) = f_0(t) + f_1(t)\\mathbf{e}_1 \\) with \\( f_0(t) \\) admissible but \\( f_1(t) = e^{t^2} \\) (not of exponential order), then \\( \\mathcal{L}\\{f\\}(s) \\) does not exist. Similarly"}}